This research paper investigates the conditions for invariant curves in a specific class of discrete-time dynamical systems defined by the equations:
$$
\begin{pmatrix} 
x_{n+1} \\ 
x_n^\alpha 
\end{pmatrix} 
= 
\mathcal{F} 
\begin{pmatrix} 
x_n \\ 
x_{n-1} 
\end{pmatrix} 
= 
\begin{pmatrix} 
f(x_n,\,x_{n-1}) \\ 
x_n 
\end{pmatrix} 
,
$$
where $0 < \alpha < 1$. We first confirm the stability of an origin at coordinates $(0,0)$ and confirm the presence of two periodic orbits around this origin. We then explore the conditions for the existence of invariant curves that enclose these periodic orbits, specifically in relation to the parameters and values of $\alpha$. Our analysis reveals that in certain parameter ranges, no such invariant curves exist, potentially leading to chaotic system behavior. We illustrate our findings using a detailed examination of the LogMap family and numerical investigations of another discrete-time system.
